MAN I LOVE CLAUDE MODS

Built another one today: usage meter. It puts your Claude 5 hour and weekly limits in the Claude Code status line, with the 5h reset time, and pops a toast at 80% and again at 90%.

Format: Claude 5h 62% (resets 18:40) · week 41%

Mods came out on Oct 1. A mod is a TypeScript function that hooks an event inside Claude Code. This one hooks session.measure, which fires after each turn and hands you rateLimits, one entry per window with percentUsed and resetsAt. The core is about this:

on('session.measure', ($, e, next) => {
  const five = e.rateLimits.find(l => l.kind === 'five_hour')
  if (five) $.ui.status(`Claude 5h ${Math.round(five.percentUsed)}%`)
  return next(e)
})

Sonnet wrote it in under 2 minutes and Opus reviewed it. Opus caught one bug: the first version remembered sent toasts by the reset timestamp, so if that timestamp moved by a second between readings, the 80% toast would come back on every turn. Now it keeps the highest warning sent per window and clears it when usage drops under 80%. 8 tests.

Second thing that bit me: I closed the session, opened a new one, and the mod was gone. Mods you hot reload live in a folder tied to that one session. To load a local mod everywhere, add its folder to CLAUDE_CODE_PLUGIN_DIRS in the env block of ~/.claude/settings.json.

Free and MIT, repo in the reply.
